Draw the **Fathom bonus drifter**, an 8-frame **sprite sheet** of a deep-sea
bonus pickup, using only the drawing tool.

The drifter is a **harmless amber jellyfish** that wanders the corridors of a
pitch-dark trench in a maze-chase game; the player grazes it for bonus points. You
are drawing a drifting glow — a glowing amber bell with a frilled skirt and swaying
tendrils — as a single directionless sway loop, **not** a predator and **not** the
player. In the game a wandering Lanternjaw predator wears this exact jellyfish as a
disguise, so draw a clean, generic amber jellyfish with nothing predatory (no jaws,
no teeth, no dark body).

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each targeting a frame
with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue are recorded to a
per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-circle --frame 0 --cx 16 --cy 12 --r 7 --color "#ffd166"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the drifter sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: a glowing amber jellyfish — a bell with a
bright core, a frilled skirt, and a few tendrils — drawn in each of its 32×32
frames, with the skirt and tendrils swaying a little from frame to frame so playing
them loops as a gentle, directionless drift. When you are satisfied, stop: the
recorded per-frame action logs are your finished submission.
